  "query_preview": "..Cool and stable conditions have built into the\nregion today as we find ourselves under the influence of a weak\nupper level ridge in the wake of a cold trough. Brief clearing\nthat occurred across the region earlier this evening is now\ndeteriorating as upper-level moisture tied to an approaching\nshortwave trough filters into the eastern Great Basin region.\nWhile we'll be under the influence of weak surface high pressure\non Sunday, middle and high-level cloud cover will remain draped\nacross the forecast area, helping to keep surface temperatures\ncooler. \n\nA weakening trough moving in from the eastern Pacific will phase\ninto Utah late sunday through Monday, though a majority of model\nsolutions show a lack of moisture and strong dynamics that would\nsupport significant snowfall across Utah. As such, would\nanticipate light snowfall across the northern Utah mountains and\nlee side valley areas on Sunday night through Monday, with minor\naccumulations at best. It's worth noting that there remains a\nsmall cohort of models that favor much more robust dynamics that\nwould favor higher intensity precipitation, not only for northern\nUtah but also for a portion of central and southern Utah. Even\nthen, total SWE associated with these more robust solutions favors\nonly around 0.1\" or less in the terrain... with around a 10%\nchance or less of occurrence.\n\nEnsembles are in good agreement on building a stout ridge over the\nregion from late Monday through at least Wednesday before any\nchance of weakening. Upstream energy over the eastern Pacific\nregion may act to supply a series of weak impulses through the\nlarge scale ridge that should (hopefully) act to limit the\npotential for persistent valley inversions... but that's about it.\nWould anticipate any energy moving through the broad ridge to\nserve up light snowfall in the high terrain, but not much else.\nThe next big storm cycle will have to wait until we see a\nbreakdown of this ridge.",
